jurassic world dominion is ready to  conquer the world and depending on who  you ask that might be a bad thing  critics have seen the jurassic park  super sequel and not everyone likes what  they saw  we made a terrible mistake when michael  crichton first wrote his novel jurassic  park back in 1990 he probably didn't  imagine that it would become a cinematic  franchise that would keep audiences  coming back for decades after the first  trilogy of movies ended with 2001's  jurassic park 3. it looked like that was  it for the likes of alan grant ellie  sattler and ian malcolm but like the  very dinosaurs themselves universal  revived the franchise in 2015 for  jurassic world which centered on an  improved supposedly safer version of the  park of course those safety measures  once again couldn't contain these  prehistoric beasts and the end of its  sequel jurassic world fallen kingdom  drastically changed the status quo of  the entire planet as dinosaurs escaped  out of a northern californian compound  to truly walk the earth this poses a  tantalizing question what would the  modern world look like if dinosaurs  roamed free obviously this idea is at  the very heart of the franchise and it's  clear that these creatures are such a  powerful force that it's almost  impossible for humans to coexist with  them jurassic world dominion is here to  escalate things even further the  trailers from the sequel promised a  globetrotting adventure with owen grady  and claire dearing racing through  different continents trying to escape  giant beasts and just to make things  more exciting sam neal laura dern and  jeff goldblum are all returning to their  roles from the original movie  thankfully the first reactions from  critics recently arrived online so let's  take a look at whether jurassic world  dominion is worth your time or if it's a  giant pile of dino dung  why do they always have to go bigger  unfortunately a lot of critics weren't  impressed by director colin trevorrow's  second jurassic world outing with many  blasting it for his simple plot and  poorly thought out character arcs  obviously the main draw here is the  dinosaurs and the carnage they're  inevitably going to cause but a  captivating human story is necessary to  make audiences care about the action  unfolding on screen gizmodo and io9  reporter jermaine lucier called it the  worst entry in the entire franchise  saying it's too long it's wildly  repetitive and the dinosaurs are window  dressing for multiple uninteresting  unrelated stories the actors do their  best but it's all just overindulgent and  pointless one of if not the worst in the  franchise it seems that many think the  story from trevorrow and derek connolly  tries to do too much but achieves  nothing by the end of the film which is  definitely disappointing meanwhile  variety in the av club critic courtney  howard tore into the film by saying it  doesn't follow the concept it's selling  the audience to instead focus on other  and less engaging storylines she tweeted  it ignores the premise it should have  had from the start instead synthetically  engineering two entirely different  uninteresting and hollow stories that it  struggles to connect this is a sentiment  echoed by thrilla's critic esther  zuckerman who simply says jurassic world  dominion is easily one of the stupidest  movies i've ever seen  see not so bad  but not everyone is feeling so negative  about the film some critics think it's a  unique adventure with interesting ideas  pandango's eric davis noted how  impressed he was with the horror-tinged  dinosaur scenes which is always great to  hear since steven spielberg's original  movie had its fair share of scares the  critic also praised the increased  presence of jeff goldblum's iconic ian  malcolm while noting that the movie  achieves in being in his words a solid  finale that's a vastly different  reaction to those calling it the worst  of the franchise but davis isn't the  only one who is 